## Summary

Previously, we skipped signature compatibility checks for `__init__` and `__new__`, even when the method was explicitly decorated with `@override`. We now report incompatible overrides while continuing to allow constructors without `@override` to change their superclass's signature.

```python
from typing import override

class Parent:
    def __init__(self, value: int) -> None: ...

class Child(Parent):
    @override
    def __init__(self, value: str) -> None: ...  # Before: no error. After: invalid-method-override.

class Undecorated(Parent):
    def __init__(self, value: str) -> None: ...  # Before and after: no error.
```

This moves [`classes_override`](https://github.com/python/typing/blob/5672d88d54c149de13d0bbd3f2995b5449f72e20/conformance/tests/classes_override.py) from Partial to Pass.

<details>
<summary>Case analysis</summary>

Changing a constructor's parameter from `int` to `str` is incompatible with the superclass's signature. We now reject both overrides, as does pyrefly. Mypy and pyright accept them (which is why they fail this part of the conformance test):

```python
from typing import Self, override

class Parent:
    def __init__(self, value: int) -> None: ...
    def __new__(cls, value: int) -> Self: ...

class Child(Parent):
    @override
    def __init__(self, value: str) -> None: ...  # ty, pyrefly: error; mypy, pyright: OK
    @override
    def __new__(cls, value: str) -> Self: ...  # ty, pyrefly: error
